"/></g><g class=\"hot\" data-info=\"Day 28, without fertilization: estrogen and progesterone fall abruptly, the ischemic functionalis separates from the basalis, and the lining and blood are sloughed off: menses.\"><path class=\"cy-sep\" d=\"M542 451H580\"/></g><text class=\"t cy-sl\" x=\"242\" y=\"410.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">straight glands</text><text class=\"t cy-sl\" x=\"382\" y=\"380.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">coiled glands</text><text class=\"t cy-sl cy-lg\" x=\"70\" y=\"404\" text-anchor=\"start\">functionalis shed</text><g class=\"cy-lg\"><g class=\"am-callout\"><path class=\"lead\" d=\"M179 413L179 445\"/><circle class=\"dot\" cx=\"179\" cy=\"445\" r=\"1.8\"/><text class=\"t cy-sl\" x=\"176\" y=\"417\" text-anchor=\"end\">spiral artery</text></g></g><text class=\"t cy-in\" x=\"422\" y=\"422\" text-anchor=\"middle\">stratum functionalis</text><text class=\"t cy-in cy-in-s\" x=\"426\" y=\"462.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">stratum basalis</text><text class=\"t cy-in cy-in-s\" x=\"426\" y=\"477.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">myometrium</text><text class=\"t cy-sl\" x=\"580\" y=\"367\" text-anchor=\"end\">arteries constrict</text></g><text class=\"t cy-h\" x=\"20\" y=\"510\" text-anchor=\"start\">UTERINE PHASES · L14 COUNTS DAYS 1-5, 5-14, 15-28</text><g class=\"hot\" data-info=\"Menstrual phase, days 1 to 5: the functionalis is shed; the basalis stays.\"><rect class=\"cy-band is-on\" x=\"20\" y=\"516\" width=\"90\" height=\"18\" rx=\"4\"/><text class=\"t cy-bt b\" x=\"65\" y=\"529\" text-anchor=\"middle\">menstrual</text></g><g class=\"hot\" data-info=\"Proliferative (or follicular) phase, days 5 to 14: estrogen from the growing follicles; the basalis rebuilds the functionalis.\"><rect class=\"cy-band\" x=\"110\" y=\"516\" width=\"190\" height=\"18\" rx=\"4\"/><text class=\"t cy-bt\" x=\"205\" y=\"529\" text-anchor=\"middle\">proliferative</text></g><g class=\"hot\" data-info=\"Secretory (or luteal) phase, days 15 to 28: progesterone from the corpus luteum; a thick functionalis whose glands secrete.\"><rect class=\"cy-band\" x=\"300\" y=\"516\" width=\"280\" height=\"18\" rx=\"4\"/><text class=\"t cy-bt\" x=\"440\" y=\"529\" text-anchor=\"middle\">secretory</text></g><path class=\"cy-axis\" d=\"M20 537H580M30 537V543M50 537V540M70 537V540M90 537V540M110 537V543M130 537V540M150 537V540M170 537V540M190 537V540M210 537V540M230 537V540M250 537V540M270 537V540M290 537V543M310 537V540M330 537V540M350 537V540M370 537V540M390 537V540M410 537V540M430 537V540M450 537V540M470 537V540M490 537V540M510 537V540M530 537V540M550 537V540M570 537V543\"/><text class=\"t cy-dn b\" x=\"30\" y=\"556\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1</text><text class=\"t cy-dn\" x=\"110\" y=\"556\" text-anchor=\"middle\">5</text><text class=\"t cy-dn\" x=\"290\" y=\"556\" text-anchor=\"middle\">14</text><text class=\"t cy-dn\" x=\"570\" y=\"556\" text-anchor=\"middle\">28</text></svg></div></div></figure><div class=\"am-controls\"><label class=\"am-slider\"><span>day 1</span><input type=\"range\" min=\"1\" max=\"28\" step=\"1\" value=\"1\" data-amk=\"d\" aria-label=\"Day of the cycle\"><span>28</span></label><button type=\"button\" class=\"am-btn\" data-amplay aria-pressed=\"false\">Play</button></div>\n<div class=\"am-controls\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"am-btn\" data-amstep=\"-1\">Back a day</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"am-btn\" data-amstep=\"1\">Next day</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"am-btn\" data-amt=\"preg\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Pregnancy</button></div><p class=\"am-summary\" aria-live=\"polite\"></p><p class=\"am-info\">Hover or tap a part of the drawing to read about it.</p><details class=\"am-sources\"><summary>What this shows, and what's simplified</summary><ul><li>From the L16 notes, slides and recording (Dr. Madison): the three parts of a typical 28-day cycle (follicular about 14 days, midcycle 14 days before menses, luteal about 14 days); LH fairly steady, then the surge; estradiol's negative feedback, the switch to positive feedback above 200 pg/mL, inhibin suppressing FSH, progesterone's negative feedback; ovulation 24 to 36 hours after the surge on day 14; the corpus luteum regressing over 14 days into the corpus albicans; menses on day 28 from the abrupt loss of estrogen and progesterone; what estrogen and progesterone do to the endometrium. From the L14 notes and slides (Dr. Herr): menstrual days 1-5, proliferative 5-14, secretory 15-28; the functionalis and basalis; straight glands, then coiled and secreting; the drop in progesterone constricting the spiral arteries; the placenta's hormone keeping the corpus luteum in pregnancy (the slides don't name it).</li><li>Curves are qualitative, relative levels with no units: their shapes follow the slides (LH steady with one surge; estradiol rising to a peak just before it; progesterone high in the luteal phase) and the standard textbook cycle diagram, and only the slide's 200 pg/mL line is a number. FSH at midcycle follows Dr. Herr's L14 graph (FSH dips a little, then peaks with LH just before ovulation, then stays low), which the hub follows (Sam, 2026-11-08); the standard textbook figure draws the same smaller FSH peak beside the LH surge (Guyton and Hall, Textbook of Medical Physiology, the female monthly cycle; Costanzo, Physiology, the menstrual cycle). In the L16 recording Dr. Madison said the same: FSH rises at midcycle, far less than LH, because inhibin preferentially inhibits it; his slide's 'FSH is suppressed by production of inhibin' says why the peak is small. Inhibin has no curve, because the slides give no levels for it across the cycle.</li><li>The FSH rise at the very end and start of the cycle, the small second estradiol rise from the corpus luteum, and where the pregnancy curves part (about day 22) are drawn from the standard diagram to make the cycle continuous; the slides give no levels for them. The ovary and endometrium are schematic, not to scale. Educational, not medical advice.</li></ul></details></div>",
